<section class="section section--cream">
  <div class="container" style="max-width:820px">
    <div class="text-center">
      <span class="section-label">How we approach forms</span>
      <h2 class="section-title" style="margin-inline:auto">Every form has one job.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="principles-grid" style="margin-top:2rem">
      <div class="principle-card">
        <h3>Ask exactly enough.</h3>
        <p>The fewer fields, the higher the conversion. We ask for the bare minimum plus any detail that lets you dispatch smarter.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="principle-card">
        <h3>Route to the right inbox.</h3>
        <p>Emergency requests flag differently than routine ones. Commercial inquiries can be routed to a dedicated address. Service-area forms can route by zip.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="principle-card">
        <h3>Spam-resistant by default.</h3>
        <p>Web3Forms handles the spam filter. No CAPTCHAs. Honeypot fields. The form stays fast and clean.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="principle-card">
        <h3>Thank-you that sells.</h3>
        <p>Every form lands on a branded thank-you page explaining what happens next, reducing "did it go through?" follow-up calls.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="principle-card">
        <h3>Mobile-first.</h3>
        <p>Most service-business forms are filled out on a phone. Ours are designed for thumbs first, then expanded for desktop.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="principle-card">
        <h3>Accessible by default.</h3>
        <p>Every input has a visible label, every error message is announced, every field meets WCAG AA contrast.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

